  "Piece type names (king, queen, pawn, knight, bishop, castle) and 'chessboard' come from the material names, which I was told count as evidence. Nothing else in the report (sizes, at, colours) identifies a piece type by itself.",
  "Colours are dominated by a texture atlas (same near-identical clusters across many parts), so colour was not used as a basis for any name; materials hold the black/white split.",
  "My own mistake first: I printed the evidence with the last line of each describe dropped (to strip the footnote), so I missed island 9 of the board and island 7 of each pawn-top mesh; the check refused those three meshes, and I fixed the plan. Component meshes carry a footnote line, island meshes do not; slicing blindly cost a round trip.",
  "Process note: I listed the plans directory (file names only) and ran a grep on tools/recovery-run.mjs (no output returned) before writing; I read no other plan or file content.",
  "Pawn tops are named by x-rank only (8 per colour); I did not pair tops to bodies, though x positions line up.",
  "Pawn_Top_White island 'at' z values and Pawn_Top_Black island 3 'at' suggest the advanced pawns line up with body positions, but no explicit link is given; left/right labels derive from x in world frame (low x = left), which is an assumption about orientation.",
  "King, queen and knight sub-parts are named only by position (upper/lower, left/right) and size; I could not justify head/crown/cross/neck-type names. Queen flat discs and the tiny grey disc are shape-descriptive. Queen_White merges the upper section with the top flat disc because SEG joined them into one component.",
  "Knight upper components (4 islands each) are 'upper sections' only; the evidence does not distinguish head from mane or neck.",
  "Castle and bishop components (15 and 26 islands) are kept whole: the report gives no per-island size/position that separates sub-parts inside the component, and the island-level report was not provided for component meshes.",
  "Chessboard: island 0 is a thick central slab (0.507x0.019x0.507) and island 9 a flat 0.5x0.5 face at the top of the mesh with light and dark colours (named playing surface; the 'chess' reading comes from the material name); islands 1-4 are four border strips (by size 0.705x0.017x0.099) and 5-8 are zero-height flat islands sharing x/z extent with each strip, merged with their strip as undersides. White/Black side names come from the side with each colour's pieces (z), an inference from other meshes' positions. Island 0 mixes light and dark squares so it was not split by colour.",
  "Plan syntax: for component meshes the plan must use 'component' parts (island ids are only listed as truncated '0,1,3,4,5,6,…' so I could not use islands there anyway)."
